ct decisions (decision quality), calm in crisis (stress), understands complicated technology (technical). Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas (communication), egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So both have communication issues, but C's is difficulty communicating ideas (maybe more severe) and egocentric, while B's is nasty remarks and pretentious. Both are concerning.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: concentration (attention), problem solving (technical), responsibility (responsibility), responds adequately to unexpected events (stress). Concerns: hot temper (stress/crew), know-it-all (communication), maybe arrogance. No explicit reliability info. So D lacks reliability evidence.\n\nCandidate A: strengths: anticipates dangerous situations (decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), spatial vision (technical), leadership (crew cooperation). Concerns: show-off (crew), not open to new ideas (adaptability). No explicit reliability or stress resilience evidence.\n\nThus B and C are strongest. Which is better? B has strong stress handling, but weak memory for numbers (attention) which is important for numeric data (e.g., fuel calculations). C has strong reliability and decision quality, but communication issues and lack of adaptability. For long-distance flights, reliability and decision quality are crucial, but communication is also critical. However, memory for numbers is also crucial. B's weak memory could be a safety risk. C's communication difficulty could also be a risk.\n\nWe could propose that we need more info on B's memory for numbers and D's crew cooperation, but we can still make a vote. Perhaps we could vote for Candidate C, emphasizing reliability and decision quality, and argue that communication can be trained, while weak memory for numbers is more fundamental. Or we could vote for Candidate B, arguing that stress handling and technical competence outweigh memory issue, which could be mitigated.\n\nWe need to bring in our own facts: B's weak memory for numbers, B's nasty remarks. D's hot temper, know-it-all. A's not open to new ideas.\n\nWe could argue that Candidate D's hot temper and know-it-all are serious, so D is out. Candidate A's lack of openness is concerning for adaptability, and show-off may affect crew cooperation, so A is less suitable. So we narrow to B vs C. Then we can argue that while B's memory for numbers is a concern, the role requires high attention to numeric data (fuel, weight). C's communication difficulty may be more severe because it affects crew coordination. So we might still favor C? Or we could say we need to ask about B's memory for numbers.\n\nWe could ask a tool question to Agent 1 about B's memory for numbers? Actually Agent 1 may have info. The shared mental model says Agent 1 has not shared B's memory fact; they have B's strengths and concerns but not memory. So we could ask Agent 1: \"Do you have any observations on Candidate B's attention to numeric details?\" But we already have that fact from our notes: B has relatively weak memory for numbers. So we can bring that in.\n\nWe could also ask Agent 1 about Candidate D's crew cooperation, but we have D's hot temper and know-it-all.\n\nWe need to produce a public message now. We should state our current vote. Let's decide to vote for Candidate C, because reliability and decision quality are top, and we think communication can be trained, while B's weak memory for numbers is a more fundamental safety issue. We'll reference our own facts: B's weak memory for numbers, B's nasty remarks, C's 100% reliability, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, but also note C's communication difficulty and egocentric. We'll argue that overall C is stronger.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: we are responding to Agent 1 and 3. We'll say we appreciate both, but I think C's reliability outweighs B's memory issue.\n\nThus public message: state vote for Candidate C, explain why.\n\nProceed