ing new perspective. We have facts about A: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized, regarded as a show\u2011off, not open to new ideas. No explicit reliability, stress, etc. We could argue that A's anticipation and leadership could be valuable, but concerns about organization and openness to criticism are serious for long-distance. Also lack of evidence on stress resilience, decision quality, etc. So A likely not best.\n\nFor D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills, hot temper, know-it-all. No explicit reliability, decision quality. Concerns about temperament and arrogance may affect crew cooperation. So D also not best.\n\nThus we can argue that after reviewing A and D, they are less suitable, reinforcing C as best. Or we could shift to B if we think D's responsibility and concentration are strong, but D lacks reliability evidence. So likely keep C.\n\nWe need to reference the discussion: Agent 2's reaffirmation of C, Agent 3's B. We can say we still support C, and add that A and D have significant concerns and lack evidence, making them weaker alternatives. Provide explicit facts: A's unorganized and not open to new ideas (adaptability), D's hot temper and arrogance (crew cooperation). Conclude that C remains best.\n\nThus produce public message: \"I agree with Agent 2, still recommend Candidate C, because A and D have serious concerns and lack evidence.\" Provide vote C.\n\nProceed."}
